Description: x11window: Show a stereo window's views at the size it declares for one view
 An X11 window's buffer is its own pixmap, so a program whose views have
 another resolution than the size they are shown at had no way to say so:
 the two 1920x540 views of a half top-and-bottom 1920x1080 video could only
 be shown at the window's size, or stretched by the program and resized
 again here.
 .
 A program that declares its window full side by side can now give the
 size one view is shown at in _KDE_NET_WM_STEREO_CONTENT_ONE_VIEW_SIZE
 (CARDINAL/32, width and height, in display pixels), as a Wayland surface's
 viewport destination does. The X11 window keeps the program's own size,
 both views at their own resolution (3840x540), its place on screen is one
 view at the declared size (1920x1080), each view is drawn at that place,
 resized once, and pointer input reaches the first view there. A new size
 changes the place; maximizing or resizing the window changes only the size
 the views are drawn at, and an interactive resize does not wait for the
 program, whose window does not change. The property is read on change and
 ignored for a declaration made by a window rule. KWin announces this as
 version 6 of _KDE_NET_WM_STEREO_CONTENT_SUPPORTED.
